Information about the DOSBox-X Debugger

Debugger interface
------------------

In builds where it is enabled, DOSBox-X supports breaking into the debugger
interface, which is shown on the console.

A mapper shortcut is provided to break into the debugger on demand. Normally
this shortcut is set to Alt+Pause (Mac: Alt+F12).

In Windows, DOSBox-X can create a console and show the debugger interface
on it.

On other systems including Linux and Mac OS X, DOSBox-X must be started
from a terminal in order to enable the debugger.

The debugger interface should scale and respond to resizing of the
terminal window.

WARNING: Fitting to the window was added in DOSBox-X. The debugger
         interface in DOSBox SVN requires a minimum terminal window
         size to function, and may segfault if the terminal is too
         small.

The debugger interface is written against the "ncurses" library.

The window regions of the debugger interface are:
 - Register Overview
 - Data view
 - Code Overview
 - Variable (not shown by default)
 - Output

The register window will show at all times the contents of the CPU
registers and segment registers as well as other important CPU
state.

Data view allows viewing the contents of memory while debugging.
The location shown is controlled by a segment:offset pair.

In DOSBox-X, the Data view also permits viewing data as a linear
(pageable) offset and as a physical memory view (outside the
CPU's paging control).

The code overview/disassembly window shows the contents of a
memory location as disassembled x86 instructions. Normally, this
is set to the instruction pointer, but it can be set anywhere.
Decoding is based on the CPU mode.

The variable list is used when the debugger is given variables
to debug by. Variable names can be used anywhere a debugger expression is
accepted. Direct call and jump targets in the code view are also annotated
when their address has a matching variable.

The output window allows you to scroll through the last 1000
or so log messages written from within the codebase by LOG()
or LOG_MSG(). If the window is scrolled to the bottom, new
messages will appear by default.

The lowest row of the terminal is reserved for a line where
the user can enter debugger commands. An underscore shows
where the cursor is positioned.

The code and data views have been fixed in DOSBox-X to indicate
when data is not available to view for a specific segment:offset
or linear address.

If the CPU is in protected mode, and the segment portion refers
to a segment that does not exist, or the offset extends past
the limit of that segment, the code or data view will show
'na' instead of a byte value.

If 80386 paging is enabled, and the segment:offset or linear
address refers to a page that is not present, then the data
view will show 'pf' to indicate this.

  na = segment does not exist, or offset exceeds segment limit

  pf = segment:offset or linear address is paged out or
       not present according to page tables.

Debugging a program and breaking on start
-----------------------------------------

DOSBox-X includes a built-in command to launch a program and
break at the entry point.

DEBUGBOX [command] [options]

You can also type DEBUGBOX without a parameter to start the
debugger.

Debugger MCP control protocol
-----------------------------

When the debugger is enabled, DOSBox-X can start a minimal TCP control
client for external debugger tooling. This is used by the companion
[dosbox-x-mcp-server](https://github.com/caiiiycuk/dosbox-x-mcp-server) project.

Enable it in the [dosbox] section:

  mcp_server=58991

DOSBox-X connects to:

  127.0.0.1:<mcp_server>

The external MCP server must be started first and listen on that address.
If the server is not available, DOSBox-X keeps running normally and retries
the connection in the background. If the connection is lost, DOSBox-X will
try to reconnect.

The default value is 0, which disables debugger MCP control.

The control channel is intentionally line-oriented text, not JSON. The MCP
server sends one request line:

  REQ <id> PING
  REQ <id> BREAK
  REQ <id> EXEC <debugger command>

DOSBox-X replies with:

  BEGIN <id> OK
  <zero or more output lines>
  END <id>

or:

  BEGIN <id> ERR
  <one or more error lines>
  END <id>

Only one debugger command is expected to be pending at a time. The MCP
server serializes calls before sending them to DOSBox-X.

PING returns PONG and is used to check the active connection.

BREAK enters the built-in debugger, equivalent to invoking the debugger
break action.

EXEC runs one existing debugger command through the same parser used by the
interactive debugger prompt. For example:

  REQ 7 EXEC CPU

returns the same CPU state text that the interactive CPU command would write
to the debugger output window.

Resume commands (`RUN`, `RUNWATCH`, and `VRT`) are acknowledged before
DOSBox-X leaves the debugger loop. Their response is only a successful
acceptance of the command, not a post-resume state snapshot.

Single-step command
-------------------

Debugger-enabled builds accept a `STEPIN` command anywhere debugger text
commands are accepted, including through the external control channel:

  REQ 8 EXEC STEPIN

`STEPIN` executes exactly one guest instruction (step into, equivalent to the
F11 key) and returns after that instruction has completed, leaving execution
stopped in the debugger. Unlike `RUN`/`RUNWATCH`/`VRT`, its response is sent
after the step has finished rather than on acceptance, and it reports the
resulting position and registers:

  DEBUG: STEPIN CS:IP=0813:0103 AX=1234 BX=0000 CX=00FF DX=0813

`STEPIN` is rejected unless the debugger is already stopped in its loop (the
whole `DEBUG:` line "requires the debugger to be stopped" is reported
otherwise). Start the program with `DEBUGBOX`, or break in first, so it is
stopped at the instruction to step. A single instruction may be a jump, a CALL
or an interrupt, so CS:IP can move non-sequentially and stepping an `INT` may
enter an interrupt handler instead of returning immediately.

`STEPIN` is step-into: it is not F10 step-over (which may execute more than one
instruction to get past a CALL) and it is not a source-line or function-level
step. The interactive display is refreshed after an external step using the
same update routines the F10/F11 keys use.

Bounded memory read command
---------------------------

Debugger-enabled builds accept a `MEMREAD` command wherever debugger text
commands are accepted, including through the external control channel:

  REQ 9 EXEC MEMREAD 0813:0100 0008

`MEMREAD <segment>:<offset> <length>` reads 1-256 bytes of conventional RAM
(real mode only) and returns them in the command response:

  DEBUG: MEMREAD 0813:0100 LEN=0008 HEX=B83412BB7856EBF8

The command is read-only: it never executes a guest instruction, never writes
guest memory or files, and does not move the observed CS:IP. It requires the
debugger to be stopped, and it refuses any request it cannot fully satisfy -
a byte that cannot be read fails the whole command instead of being replaced
by a zero, so a returned `00` always means the byte really is zero.

Limitations: this is an observation of ordinary conventional RAM below
`A0000h` in real mode only. Protected-mode execution, selector-based or
otherwise translated addresses, upper memory, EMS/XMS and ranges that would
wrap the 20-bit address space or depend on A20 behaviour are rejected. The
request must not cross a 64 KiB segment boundary. Reading conventional RAM is
not assumed to be universally side-effect-free for every machine or device, so
the command is intentionally restricted to this bounded range.

Output is captured from DEBUG_ShowMsg while an MCP command is running. Paged
debugger output is not paused for terminal input during this capture, so
commands such as HELP, GDT, LDT, IDT, and DOS memory listings can be returned
to the MCP client without hanging on the debugger pager.

The MCP control queue is polled both from the normal timer path and directly
from the active debugger loop. This lets MCP clients issue commands after a
BREAK has stopped emulation in the debugger; concurrent MCP tool calls are
still serialized by the MCP server before they reach DOSBox-X.

Unknown commands return ERR and include the normal debugger
"command not recognized" text. Commands behind build options, such as
C_HEAVY_DEBUG-only commands, may be unavailable in some builds. MCP clients
should call HELP for the live command list from the running DOSBox-X build,
or use their own static command catalog as a hint before falling back to raw
EXEC.

Save state commands
-------------------

Debugger-enabled builds accept `SAVESTATE` and `LOADSTATE` wherever debugger
text commands are accepted, including through the external control channel:

  REQ 10 EXEC SAVESTATE 3
  REQ 11 EXEC LOADSTATE /path/to/game.sav

The argument is a save slot, 1-100, or a file. An argument made only of digits
is always a slot; anything else is a file name, taken exactly as typed (spaces
included, with no quotes around it) and relative to the working directory
unless absolute. The name must end in lowercase `.sav` and contain `.sav`
nowhere else: the flagged files of FLAGSAVE are written beside the state under
the same name with its first `.sav` made `.dat`: a name without `.sav` would
put them over the state itself, and one with an earlier `.sav` would send them
elsewhere. With no argument the current slot is used, even when the menu saves
to a file. The outcome is in the command response:

  DEBUG: Saved slot 3
  DEBUG: Loaded /path/to/game.sav

These commands are for a front end with nobody at the window: the save state
code opens no dialog for them. An error that the menu would show in a message
box is logged instead, a version, program, memory or machine mismatch that the
menu would ask about is refused (unless `forceloadstate` is set), and no save
remark is asked for. A failure is reported as one (`DEBUG: SAVESTATE failed:
...`), and the log says why. A refusal or a failure answers ERR on the control
channel, and, as for any command that returns an error, the response ends with
the `*** Debugger command not recognized` line. A load that fails part-way, on
a state missing a part, can leave the machine partly restored, as the menu's
would.

Both require the debugger to be stopped, so break in first, and neither runs
from a timer tick: while the guest runs, the control channel is served from a
tick handler, and loading a state there would replace the tick handler list
the timer is walking. A command refused for that can be sent again once the
stop is reached.

Presence
--------

Debugger-enabled builds accept a `PRESENCE` command wherever debugger text
commands are accepted. A client driving the emulator over the control channel
marks with it the window it drives, so whoever sits at the host can tell that
somebody is driving it and keep their hands off:

  REQ 12 EXEC PRESENCE acting rgb=FF8C00 edge=0.025 steps=6 acting=0.850 held=0.283
         fade=2.5 outline=2A1400 sprite=X.../XX../XoX./XXXX pointer=1
         caption=send_keys%20Esc serial=3 tint=FF8C00 keys=Esc place=auto

(one line). The first word is the state:

  off      nothing is drawn
  held     a dim edge on the inner sides of the picture
  acting   the same edge, brighter; going back to held, it fades over `fade`
           seconds
  paused   held's edge and a pause mark: the client holds the machine stopped

The `key=value` words set what is drawn and how, and a key left out keeps its
value:

  rgb       the colour, six hex digits
  edge      the edge's width, a fraction of the picture's shorter side
  steps     the bands its gradient is drawn in, strongest at the rim
  acting    the alpha while acting, 0 to 1
  held      the alpha while held or paused, 0 to 1
  fade      the seconds from acting back to held
  sprite    an arrow drawn at the guest's mouse position, its rows top first
            and separated by `/`: `X` the outline, `o` the fill, `.` untouched
  outline   the arrow's outline colour, six hex digits (the fill is `rgb`)
  glide     the seconds the arrow takes to ease out to a new place, 0 to jump
  pointer   1 to draw the arrow (with a sprite given), 0 not to
  layers    the layers to draw, comma separated, from `edge`, `pointer`,
            `keys`, `calls`, `pause`, `record`; all of them unless told
  place     where the caption, the keycaps and the pause mark go: `top` or
            `bottom` band of the picture, or `auto`, the band showing less
  caption   the call's caption, at most 160 bytes, one word: `%XX` for a
            space, a percent sign or any byte outside printable ASCII; empty
            to take it away (it leaves with its fade)
  serial    the caption's number: a caption is new when its serial changes, so
            the same caption sent again restarts nothing; with no serial ever
            sent, every `caption` is a new one
  tint      the caption box's colour, six hex digits
  keys      the names of the keys down now, comma separated, at most 8 of at
            most 12 bytes each, encoded as `caption`; empty for none
  rec       what the client records: `none`, `video`, `audio` or `both`
  caption_text_rgb, keycap_rgb, keycap_text_rgb
            the caption's text, the keycaps' box and text, six hex digits
  caption_alpha, keycap_alpha
            the boxes' alphas, 0 to 1
  caption_in, caption_out
            the seconds a caption takes to fade in and out
  scroll_cps
            characters a second a caption too wide for its room scrolls at
  record_rgb
            the record mark's dot, six hex digits
  record_blink_s
            the record mark's blink, 0.1 to 10 seconds: shown for the first
            half, hidden for the second

Each sprite cell and each glyph pixel is one guest pixel, scaled with the
picture, so a sprite the size of the guest's own pointer is drawn at that size
whatever the mode, the scaler or the window. The arrow follows the built-in
INT 33h driver's position, within the range the driver keeps it in, gliding to
each new place from where it was drawn rather than jumping; with no driver
installed there is no arrow. The glide is drawing only: the guest's pointer is
where the driver says at once.

The caption, the keycaps and the pause mark share a band, the top or bottom
eighth of the picture, inset from the edge: the pause mark at the left, the
keycaps at the right, the caption between them, centred when it fits and
scrolling when it does not. Text is drawn in the VGA ROM font, 8x16, or 8x8 in a
band too short for it. With `place=auto` the overlay samples the guest's own
picture once a second on a grid in each band (16 columns by 2 rows, the band an
eighth of the picture), scores each band by how many samples differ from its most
common colour, and moves to the other band once that one scored 4 lower on 2
samples running.

While `rec` is not `none`, the record mark sits in the top right corner of the
picture, inset from the edge, whatever `place` says: a box of the keycaps'
colours holding a dot in `record_rgb` and `REC` for video, a dot and the VGA
font's double note for audio, both side by side for `both`. It blinks with the
period `record_blink_s` gives, counted from the last change of `rec`, so a new
recording shows at once. A band at the top keeps the room for it in both halves
of the blink, so the keycaps and the caption never move with it. A blink
repaints the mark's box alone: on the OpenGL and Direct3D 9 outputs it uploads
that box and sends none of the frame.

PRESENCE is answered at once, whether the guest runs or the debugger holds it
(it marks the window, never the guest), with one line:

  PRESENCE held frames=1234 pointer=1 output=640x400x32 px_read=51245 px_written=51744
           bytes_presented=1026560 uploads=0 upload_bytes=0
           layers=edge,pointer,keys,calls,pause,record place=bottom

(one line). `frames` counts the frames the overlay was drawn on, and `output` is
the output it is drawn on: `none` while there is no output yet, `undrawable`
for one it cannot draw on (an 8-bit output, or the TrueType output while it
shows text; its graphics modes are drawn on like any other). On an output it
draws on, counters say what drawing it has cost since that output was made
(they start again at 0 each time the render makes the output again, a new size,
format, scaler or window mode, and `frames` does not), the same on any host: `px_read` the output pixels it read (the ones
it saves before drawing, and the guest pixels `auto` samples), `px_written` the
output pixels it wrote (put back or blended), `bytes_presented` the bytes it
handed on to be presented, the scaler's own rows included, and `uploads` and
`upload_bytes` the rectangles uploaded to the overlay's own texture and their
bytes, 0 where it is drawn into the frame. On the OpenGL and Direct3D 9 outputs
the overlay is that texture, so `px_read` counts only `auto`'s samples and `px_written` and
`bytes_presented` stay 0. `layers` names the layers asked for and `place` the band in use; on an
output it cannot draw on, `layers` is empty and `lacks` names the layers asked
for. Two replies with no output made between them give the cost between them. An unknown
state or key, or a value out of its range, answers ERR naming it and changes
nothing; the response ends with that reason alone. The arguments keep their
case.

The bare command, `PRESENCE` with no state, changes nothing and answers the same line
with `version=3` appended, so a client can refuse a build older than it expects
(version 1 was implicit, version 2 drew the edge and the arrow only).

The overlay is drawn after the frame was handed to capture: screenshots, video
capture and the VGA memory never contain it. Its layers are composited once per
change into a buffer of their own. On the OpenGL and Direct3D 9 outputs that
buffer is a texture of its own, drawn as a second quad over the frame's with
every swap of the window, and only its rectangles that changed are uploaded; the
frame's pixels are never read or written for it, and a change over a still
picture shows the frame as it stands with the new overlay over it, sending none
of the frame again. On Direct3D 9 a frame the scaler is still writing is never
presented early, so a change made while the debugger holds the machine in the
middle of one shows with that frame's own present; a lost device or a resize
makes the texture again, by one whole upload. On every other output it is drawn into the scaled output just before it
is presented, and a frame blends that buffer onto the picture only where it has
to. The scaler writes the output in place, rewriting only the blocks of a line
that changed, so before it writes a line the overlay puts the picture back on
that line's rows, and after the frame it draws itself again on those rows; every
other row keeps the overlay as it was, and only a layer that changed (a fade, the
arrow moving, a new caption, a scroll step) is redrawn and presented, and on an
output that presents rectangles (the surface output) only its rectangles are. The complex scalers (hq2x, the SaI
family, advmame) and a frame that rewrites everything take the overlay off every
row first. A still overlay over a still picture costs nothing but a check per
frame. While the debugger holds the machine, its loop repaints the window when the
overlay changes, and only then.

Debugger keyboard shortcuts
---------------------------

Tab/Shift+Tab       Switch to the next/previous window
F3/F6               Previous command in history
F4/F7               Next command in history
F5                  Resume emulation
F8                  Toggle printable characters display in data view
F9                  Set/clear breakpoint
F10                 Single step (over)
F11                 Single step (into)
Up arrow            Scroll up one line (if applicable)
Down arrow          Scroll down one line (if applicable)
Left arrow          Move cursor left in command line
Right arrow         Move cursor right in command line
Page Up/Fn+Up       Scroll up by window height (if applicable)
Page Down/Fn+Down   Scroll down by window height (if applicable)
Home/Fn+Left        Scroll to top (in Output window)
End/Fn+Right        Scroll to bottom (in Output window)
Insert              Toggle insert/overwrite mode in command line
Alt+D               Set data view to DS:SI
Alt+E               Set data view to ES:DI
Alt+X               Set data view to DS:DX
Alt+B               Set data view to ES:BX
Alt+S               Set data view to SS:SP

Debugger commands
-----------------

MOVEWINDN                                   Move current window down
MOVEWINDU                                   Move current window up
SHOWWIN <winname>                           Show window (by name)
HIDEWIN <winname>                           Hide window (by name)
MEMDUMP <seg>:<off> <bytecount> [filename]  Dump memory to file (default: MEMDUMP.TXT)
MEMDUMPBIN <seg>:<off> <bytecount> [filename] Dump memory to file (default: MEMDUMP.BIN)
MEMFIND <seg> <off> [bytes in hex]          Start a memory find search instance
MEMS <operator> <value>                     Search a value within the find instance
IV <seg> <off> <name>                       Insert variable
SV <name>                                   Save variables to <name>
LV <name>                                   Load variables from <name>
SR <reg> <value>                            Set register <reg> value to <value>
SM <seg> <off> [bytes in hex]               Set memory at <seg>:<off> to byte values given
SMV <off> [bytes in hex]                    Set memory at linear/virtual <off> to byte values
FM <seg> <off>                              Freeze (lock) memory value at <seg>:<off>
EV <value> [value] ...                      Show register/expression value(s)
BP <seg> <off>                              Add breakpoint (real mode)
BPM <seg> <off>                             Add breakpoint (protected mode)
BPPM <seg> <off>                            Add memory-change breakpoint (protected mode)
BPLM <offset>                               Add breakpoint (linear/virtual address)
BPINT <intnum>                              Add breakpoint on interrupt
BPINT <intnum> <value>                      Add breakpoint on interrupt and AH=<value>
BPLIST                                      List breakpoints
BPDEL <breakpoint number>                   Delete breakpoint
RUN                                         Resume emulation
RUNWATCH                                    Resume emulation, but show state while running
SAVESTATE [slot|file]                       Save a state to a slot (1-100) or a .sav file
LOADSTATE [slot|file]                       Load a state from a slot (1-100) or a .sav file
A20                                         Show A20 gate state
A20 ON                                      Turn on A20 gate
A20 OFF                                     Turn off A20 gate
PIC                                         Show interrupt controller state
PIC MASKIRQ <irq>                           Mask IRQ at interrupt controller
PIC UNMASKIRQ <irq>                         Unmask IRQ at interrupt controller
PIC ACKIRQ <irq>                            Acknowledge IRQ at interrupt controller
PIC LOWERIRQ <irq>                          Manually lower interrupt signal
PIC RAISEIRQ <irq>                          Manually raise interrupt signal
INP/INW/IND <port>                          I/O port read byte/word/dword
OUTP/OUTW/OUTD <port> <data>                I/O port write byte/word/dword
C <seg> <off>                               Set code view to address
D <seg> <off>                               Set data view to address (segment:offset)
DV <offset>                                 Set data view to address (linear/virtual address)
DP <offset>                                 Set data view to address (physical)
LOG <hexadecimal count>                     Log CPU state, for the specified number of instructions, to LOGCPU.TXT
LOGS <hexadecimal count>                    Log CPU state, short log, to LOGCPU.TXT
LOGL <hexadecimal count>                    Log CPU state, long log, to LOGCPU.TXT
LOGC <hexadecimal count>                    Log CPU state, CS:IP-only log, to LOGCPU.TXT
ADDLOG <message>                            Add a message line to the CPU log file
INTT <intnum>                               Trace interrupt
INT <intnum>                                Start interrupt
CALLBACKS                                   Show callbacks of interrupts.
SELINFO <n>                                 Show selector information
DOS MCBS                                    Dump DOS kernel MCB chain (conventional memory allocation chain)
DOS KERN                                    Dump DOS kernel memory allocation list
DOS XMS                                     Dump XMS (extended memory) allocation list
DOS EMS                                     Dump EMS (expanded memory) allocation list
DOS FNKEY                                   Dump PC-98 function key (FnKey) mapping
BIOS MEM                                    Dump BIOS allocation and layout list
GDT                                         Dump GDT (global descriptor table)
LDT                                         Dump LDT (local descriptor table)
IDT                                         Dump IDT (interrupt descriptor table)
PAGING                                      Dump page table information
CPU                                         Dump additional CPU information
FPU                                         Dump additional FPU information
MMX [=t] <reg> [SET <val>]                  Display or set MMX registers (t=B,W,D,Q)
SSE [=t] <reg> [SET <val>]                  Display or set SSE regs (t=B,W,D,Q,X,S,F)
VRD                                         Redraw video output
VGA cmd                                     VGA related debugging commands.
PC98 cmd                                    PC98 related debugging commands.
EMU MEM/MACHINE                             Show emulator memory or machine info.
INTVEC <file>                               Dump interrupt vector table to <file>
INTHAND <intnum>                            Set code view to start of interrupt handler
EXTEND                                      Toggle additional information
TIMERIRQ                                    Start timer IRQ
VRT                                         Run, then enter debugger at next vertical retrace
TIME <time>                                 Display or change the internal time
DATE <date>                                 Display or change the internal date
HEAVYLOG                                    Toggle heavy CPU logging
ZEROPROTECT                                 Toggle zero protection
HELP                                        Show some debugger commands for reference
